1. LeetCode 124 – Binary Tree Maximum Path Sum

Difficulty: Hard

Concepts Covered:

Depth-First Search (DFS)

Recursion

Maximum Gain

Tree Traversal

Approach:

For each node, calculate the maximum gain from its left and right subtrees. Ignore negative gains. Update the global maximum with the path passing through the current node, and return the maximum one-sided gain to the parent.

class Solution {
    int maxSum = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

    public int maxPathSum(TreeNode root) {
        findMax(root);
        return maxSum;
    }

    private int findMax(TreeNode node) {
        if (node == null) return 0;

        int left = Math.max(0, findMax(node.left));
        int right = Math.max(0, findMax(node.right));

        maxSum = Math.max(maxSum, node.val + left + right);

        return node.val + Math.max(left, right);
    }
}

Time Complexity: O(n) – Each node is visited once.

Space Complexity: O(h) – Recursive stack, where h is the height of the tree.

2. LeetCode 98 – Validate Binary Search Tree

Difficulty: Medium

Concepts Covered:

DFS

Recursion

BST Properties

Range Validation

Approach:

Validate each node against a minimum and maximum range. The left subtree must contain values smaller than the current node, and the right subtree must contain values greater than the current node. Using long avoids boundary issues with Integer.MIN_VALUE and Integer.MAX_VALUE.

class Solution {
    public boolean isValidBST(TreeNode root) {
        return validate(root, Long.MIN_VALUE, Long.MAX_VALUE);
    }

    private boolean validate(TreeNode node, long min, long max) {
        if (node == null) return true;

        if (node.val <= min || node.val >= max) {
            return false;
        }

        return validate(node.left, min, node.val)
            && validate(node.right, node.val, max);
    }
}

Time Complexity: O(n) – Each node is visited once.

Space Complexity: O(h) – Recursive stack.

3. LeetCode 230 – Kth Smallest Element in a BST

Difficulty: Medium

Concepts Covered:

Inorder Traversal

DFS

Recursion

BST Properties

Approach:

Inorder traversal of a BST produces values in ascending order. Count the nodes during traversal and stop when the kth node is reached.

class Solution {
    int count = 0;
    int answer = 0;

    public int kthSmallest(TreeNode root, int k) {
        inorder(root, k);
        return answer;
    }

    private void inorder(TreeNode node, int k) {
        if (node == null) return;

        inorder(node.left, k);

        count++;
        if (count == k) {
            answer = node.val;
            return;
        }

        if (count < k) {
            inorder(node.right, k);
        }
    }
}

Time Complexity: O(n) – Worst case.

Space Complexity: O(h) – Recursive stack.

4. LeetCode 235 – Lowest Common Ancestor of a Binary Search Tree

Difficulty: Medium

Concepts Covered:

BST Properties

Recursion

Tree Traversal

Approach:

Use the BST property to locate the Lowest Common Ancestor (LCA).

If both nodes are smaller than the current node, move left.

If both nodes are larger, move right.

Otherwise, the current node is the LCA.

class Solution {
    public TreeNode lowestCommonAncestor(
        TreeNode root, TreeNode p, TreeNode q
    ) {
        if (root == null) return null;

        if (p.val < root.val && q.val < root.val) {
            return lowestCommonAncestor(root.left, p, q);
        }

        if (p.val > root.val && q.val > root.val) {
            return lowestCommonAncestor(root.right, p, q);
        }

        return root;
    }
}

Time Complexity: O(h) – Depends on the height of the tree.

Space Complexity: O(h) – Recursive stack.

5. LeetCode 450 – Delete Node in a BST

Difficulty: Medium

Concepts Covered:

BST Properties

Recursion

Tree Manipulation

Successor Node

Approach:

Find the node to delete.

If the node has no children, return null.

If it has one child, return that child.

If it has two children, find the smallest node in the right subtree (inorder successor), replace the node's value, and delete the successor.

class Solution {
    public TreeNode deleteNode(TreeNode root, int key) {
        if (root == null) return null;

        if (key < root.val) {
            root.left = deleteNode(root.left, key);
        } else if (key > root.val) {
            root.right = deleteNode(root.right, key);
        } else {
            if (root.left == null) return root.right;
            if (root.right == null) return root.left;

            TreeNode successor = findMin(root.right);
            root.val = successor.val;
            root.right = deleteNode(root.right, successor.val);
        }

        return root;
    }

    private TreeNode findMin(TreeNode node) {
        while (node.left != null) {
            node = node.left;
        }
        return node;
    }
}

Time Complexity: O(h) – Searching and deleting depend on the tree height.

Space Complexity: O(h) – Recursive stack.

